/-!
# Finite-open constant generators for constructible finite-field sheaves

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Expose XIII, 3.7.2, p. 203
(blueprint: lem-per-constructible-finite-open-constant-generation).

The smallest full subcategory is expressed by its universal property: every object
property closed under the four specified operations and containing the generators
contains each constructible sheaf. Finite coproducts include the empty coproduct.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Etale

/-- Every constructible finite-field module sheaf on a Noetherian scheme lies in the
subcategory generated by finite pushforwards of constants extended by zero from nonempty
opens in integral schemes, under isomorphisms, finite sums, extensions and direct summands.

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Expose XIII, 3.7.2, p. 203
(blueprint: lem-per-constructible-finite-open-constant-generation).

Proof (the DAG's finite-ring filtration and Noetherian induction argument):
1. Fix $B$ and $S$, and denote the subcategory in the statement by $\mathcal C_S$. If $h:Z\to S$
   is finite, its direct image is additive and exact by [lemma-weil-pbc-finite-direct-image-
   exact]; this assertion for $B$-modules follows on forgetting scalars, since kernels and
   cokernels of module maps are formed with their induced scalar actions. It therefore preserves
   finite sums, extensions and retract diagrams. On generators, the section formula in [def-weil-
   pbc-direct-image] identifies $h_*(q_*k_!B)=(hq)_*k_!B$, and [lem-weil-pbc-finite-composition]
   says $hq$ is finite. Hence $h_*$ takes any finite construction from the specified generators
   over $Z$ to such a construction over $S$.
2. For each closed subset $T\subseteq |S|$, give $T$ its reduced induced closed subscheme
   structure $i_T:T_{\rm red}\to S$ using [lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme]. Prove
   by induction on proper closed subsets that $i_{T*}G\in\mathcal C_S$ for every constructible
   $B$-module sheaf $G$ on $T_{\rm red}$. The induction is legitimate by [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-
   scheme-closed-subsets-minimal]: a nonempty collection of failures has a minimal closed support.
   The empty support gives the zero sheaf, the empty finite sum. Closed subschemes here are
   Noetherian by [lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-finite] and [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-
   noetherian]. It is enough to establish the induction step for nonempty $T$.
3. Write $R=T_{\rm red}$. Its finitely many irreducible components are supplied by [lem-weil-pbc-
   noetherian-finitely-many-components]. The definition [def-weil-pbc-etale-constructible-sheaf]
   supplies a finite locally closed partition adapted to $G$, with reduced strata allowed. For
   each component, its generic point lies in one stratum, and that locally closed stratum contains
   a nonempty open of the component: its closed part contains the generic point and hence the
   whole component. Remove all other components and shrink to an affine open in this stratum. This
   yields pairwise disjoint nonempty affine opens $U_b\subseteq R$, one in each component, on each
   of which $G$ is finite locally constant as a $B$-module. Their union is dense. The reduced
   induced closed component $C_b\subseteq R$ exists by [lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-
   subscheme] and is integral by [def-pc-integral-scheme]; $U_b$ is an open subscheme of $C_b$.
4. Let $\ell=\operatorname{char}B$, a prime, and apply [lem-weil-pbc-etale-lisse-finite-etale-
   filtration] with $X=U_b$, $\Lambda=B$, $n=1$ and $F=G|_{U_b}$. All hypotheses hold: $U_b$ is
   affine Noetherian, $B$ is finite, the fibres of $F$ are finite and $\ell F=0$. We obtain a
   finite etale surjection $g_b:T_b\to U_b$ of constant degree $d_b\ge1$ prime to $\ell$. The
   pullback $g_b^{-1}F$ has, on each of finitely many open-and-closed pieces of $T_b$, a finite
   filtration by $B$-submodule sheaves with constant finite $B$-module quotients.
5. Apply [lem-per-finite-etale-integral-models-after-shrinking] to $C_b$, $U_b$ and $g_b$. Replace
   $U_b$ by the resulting nonempty affine open $W_b$ and restrict the cover to $v_b:V_b\to W_b$.
   Keep these shrunken opens throughout the rest of the induction step. Each $W_b$ still meets the
   generic point of its component, so $W=\coprod_bW_b$ is open dense in $R$. The cited node gives
   a finite disjoint family of integral $Y_{ba}\to C_b$ with
   $V_b=\coprod_a(Y_{ba}\times_{C_b}W_b)$. Composing with the closed immersion $C_b\to R$ gives
   finite maps $q_{ba}:Y_{ba}\to R$ by [lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-finite] and [lem-weil-pbc-
   finite-composition]. The square for $q_b:\coprod_aY_{ba}\to R$ and $W_b\hookrightarrow R$ is
   cartesian, with upper open $V_b$.
6. Restriction to $V_b$ preserves the finite filtrations of Step 4 by the exact constant-
   coefficient inverse image [lem-pe-constant-left-module-inverse-image]. Intersect each
   filtration piece with each integral open component $V_{ba}\subseteq V_b$. An irreducible space
   cannot meet two disjoint nonempty clopen pieces, so each $V_{ba}$ lies in one of the original
   clopen pieces. Its successive quotients are therefore constant finite-dimensional $B$-vector
   spaces. Choosing a basis identifies each such quotient with a finite sum of $B_{V_{ba}}$; a
   basis flag refines it into rank-one constant quotients. The elementary finite-dimensional
   argument uses only that $B$ is a field.
7. By [lem-weil-pbc-finite-etale-trace-degree], restriction followed by trace for $v_b$ is
   $d_b\,\mathrm{id}_F$ on $W_b$, with the same degree $d_b$: every geometric fibre is a fibre of
   the original $g_b$. Naturality of trace in [def-weil-pbc-finite-etale-trace] applied to each
   scalar endomorphism $m_c$ ($c\in B$) shows
   $\operatorname{tr}\,v_*v^{-1}(m_c)=m_c\operatorname{tr}$, so trace is $B$-linear. The unit is
   also $B$-linear. As $\ell\nmid d_b$, $d_b^{-1}\operatorname{tr}$ is a retraction of $F\to
   v_{b*}v_b^{-1}F$ in $B$-module sheaves. Thus $F$ is a retract of the latter, with no use of
   closure under quotients or subobjects.
8. Put $j_b:W_b\hookrightarrow R$ and $k_b:V_b\hookrightarrow Y_b=\coprod_aY_{ba}$. The finite-
   open isomorphism [lemma-weil-pbc-finite-pushforward-extension-zero] gives $j_{b!}v_{b*}H\cong
   q_{b*}k_{b!}H$. Although stated for abelian sheaves, it is $B$-linear on module sheaves:
   naturality for every multiplication $m_c$ makes the comparison commute with each constant
   scalar, and the induced scalar actions are sheaf-local. Exactness of [lem-weil-pbc-extension-
   by-zero-exact] and [lemma-weil-pbc-finite-direct-image-exact] sends the filtrations of Step 6
   to filtrations of $q_{b*}k_{b!}v_b^{-1}F$. Disjoint components give finite direct sums
   (sections are finite products, equal to finite sums of modules), and every successive quotient
   is a finite sum of $q_{ba*}k_{ba!}B_{V_{ba}}$. Each $Y_{ba}$ is integral and $V_{ba}$ nonempty
   open, so these are exactly allowed generators over $R$.
9. Step 8 constructs $j_{b!}v_{b*}v_b^{-1}F$ by finitely many sums and extensions from generators.
   Applying the additive functor $j_{b!}$ to the split pair of Step 7 exhibits $j_{b!}F$ as a
   retract. Step 1, applied to the finite closed immersion $i_T$, therefore places $i_{T*}j_{b!}F$
   in $\mathcal C_S$. Finite sums over $b$ place $i_{T*}j_!j^{-1}G$ in $\mathcal C_S$, where
   $j:W\hookrightarrow R$; the identification with the sum follows from the extension-by-zero
   stalk formula [lem-weil-pbc-extension-by-zero-stalks] and the disjointness of the $W_b$, or
   directly from its direct-sum presheaf construction.
10. Let $a:Z\hookrightarrow R$ be the reduced closed complement of $W$. The ordinary open-closed
   triangle [lem-cbc-open-closed-recollement] applied to $G[0]$ gives, on degree-zero cohomology,
   the short exact sequence $0\to j_!j^{-1}G\to G\to a_*a^{-1}G\to0$, since all three functors in
   that triangle are exact sheaf functors. The sheaf $a^{-1}G$ is constructible: [lem-weil-pbc-
   constructible-etale-set-pullback] gives its finite underlying constructible stratification, and
   the module-local trivializations are obtained by pulling back the defining trivializations of
   $G$ using [lem-pe-constant-left-module-inverse-image]. The closed subset $|Z|$ is proper in $T$
   since $W$ is dense nonempty. The induction hypothesis gives $(i_Ta)_*a^{-1}G\in\mathcal C_S$.
   Apply the exact $i_{T*}$ to this sequence; its left term is in $\mathcal C_S$ by Step 9 and its
   right term by induction, hence its middle term $i_{T*}G$ is in $\mathcal C_S$.
11. Finally let $r:S_{\rm red}\to S$ be the reduction. It is a finite closed immersion and a
   universal homeomorphism by [lem-weil-pbc-reduction-universal-homeomorphism] and [lem-weil-pbc-
   closed-immersion-finite]. The equivalence [prop-weil-pbc-universal-homeomorphism-topos] gives
   the adjunction isomorphism $F\cong r_*r^{-1}F$. This is an isomorphism of $B$-modules: the
   inverse-image equivalence transports addition and each scalar map, and its unit is natural in
   those maps. The pullback is constructible by [lem-weil-pbc-constructible-etale-set-pullback]
   and the same defining module trivializations as in Step 10. The induction result with $T=|S|$
   puts $r_*r^{-1}F$ in $\mathcal C_S$. Thus $F$ belongs to $\mathcal C_S$. The induction uses
   proper closed supports and finitely many covers, filtration steps and components at each step;
   equivalently the minimal-counterexample proof establishes membership in the closure defined by
   finite constructions. No infinite operation is used.
No closure under arbitrary subobjects or quotients is used. All operations have finite
arity, so the universal property is precisely the closure under finite constructions. -/
theorem constructible_finite_open_constant_generation
    (S : Scheme.{u}) [IsNoetherian S] (B : Type (u + 1)) [Field B] [Finite B]
    (F : Sheaf S.smallEtaleTopology (ModuleCat.{u + 1} B))
    (hF : Scheme.IsConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf F)
    (P : ObjectProperty (Sheaf S.smallEtaleTopology (ModuleCat.{u + 1} B)))
    [P.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms] [P.IsClosedUnderFiniteCoproducts]
    [P.IsClosedUnderExtensions] [P.IsStableUnderRetracts]
    (hgen : ∀ (S' W : Scheme.{u}) (π : S' ⟶ S) [IsFinite π] [IsIntegral S']
      (k : W ⟶ S') [IsOpenImmersion k] [Nonempty W],
      P ((lowerStar (ModuleCat.{u + 1} B) π).obj
        ((openImmersionLowerShriek (ModuleCat.{u + 1} B) k).obj
          ((constantSheaf W.smallEtaleTopology (ModuleCat.{u + 1} B)).obj
            (ModuleCat.of B B))))) :
    P F := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Etale
